  "name": "Androstenedione",
  "code": "A4",
  "cost": 0,
  "interpretation": "<table><thead><tr><th><div>Androstenedione (A4) Level (ng/dL or nmol/L)</div></th><th><div>Interpretation</div></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><div>Normal Levels</div></td><td><div>Normal A4 levels can vary based on age, sex, and the laboratory's reference range. In adult females, normal levels are typically between 85 to 275 ng/dL (or 2.96 to 9.55 nmol/L). In adult males, normal levels are usually between 40 to 150 ng/dL (or 1.39 to 5.22 nmol/L). Normal levels indicate that the adrenal and gonadal glands are functioning properly, and there are no significant hormonal imbalances.</div></td></tr><tr><td><div>Elevated Levels</div></td><td><div>Elevated A4 levels can be seen in conditions such as polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS) in women, congenital adrenal hyperplasia (CAH), and certain adrenal tumors. In men, elevated A4 levels may indicate conditions affecting testosterone production or metabolism. Further testing and evaluation are required to determine the underlying cause of the elevation and guide appropriate management.</div></td></tr><tr><td><div>Low Levels</div></td><td><div>Low A4 levels are relatively rare but can be seen in conditions affecting adrenal gland function, such as adrenal insufficiency. In females, low A4 levels may also be associated with primary ovarian insufficiency (premature menopause). Additional testing and clinical assessment are necessary to identify the cause of the low A4 levels.</div></td></tr></tbody></table><p><br></p>",
